<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Religion and Power</h1><p class="path-tagline">How rulers adopted, promoted and imposed faiths, and how faith in turn shaped their states.</p><p class="path-intro">Empires have rarely been neutral about belief. Some rulers embraced a new faith and spread it across their realms; others used religion to claim legitimacy, to unite diverse subjects or to set themselves apart from rivals. This path moves from Ashoka's dhamma, Constantine's patronage of the Church and Ezana's conversion, through the first caliphs and the crowning of Charlemagne, to the crusades, Safavid Shi'ism and Akbar's policy of peace with all, showing faith as both foundation and fault line.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Thinker</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/maurya-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 322 BCE – 185 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Maurya Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">After the bloody Kalinga War, Ashoka renounced conquest by force, and royal patronage helped spread Buddhism across the subcontinent and beyond.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/edicts-of-ashoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edicts-of-ashoka.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Work</span><span class="path-step-name">Edicts of Ashoka</span><span class="path-step-why">Inscribed on rocks and pillars, the edicts proclaimed dhamma and welfare measures, even naming Hellenistic kings as recipients of Ashoka's missions.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-milan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edict-of-milan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Event · 313 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Edict of Milan</span><span class="path-step-why">The agreement of 313 between Constantine and Licinius that set a persecuted sect on the road to becoming Rome's dominant institution.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/aksumite-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 100 CE – 940 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Aksumite Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">King Ezana's adoption of Christianity in the fourth century made the church a major pillar of Aksumite government.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 632 CE – 661 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Rashidun Caliphate</span><span class="path-step-why">The first caliphs, successors to the Prophet Muhammad, turned a religious community into an empire reaching Syria, Egypt and Iraq.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/coronation-of-charlemagne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coronation-of-charlemagne.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Event · 800 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Coronation of Charlemagne</span><span class="path-step-why">On Christmas Day 800 Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne in Rome, sealing the alliance between the papacy and the Frankish monarchy.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/crusades"><span class="path-step-ph">7</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Event</span><span class="path-step-name">Crusades</span><span class="path-step-why">Centuries of holy war in the name of faith, including the irony of 1204, when crusaders sacked Christian Constantinople.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/safavid-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · 1501 CE – 1736 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Safavid Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Ismail I proclaimed Twelver Shia Islam the state religion in 1501, forging an Iranian identity distinct from his Sunni Ottoman and Uzbek neighbours.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/mughal-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taj-mahal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 1526 CE – 1857 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Mughal Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Akbar's Sulh-i-kul, or 'peace with all', and his abolition of the jizya in 1564 contrast with Aurangzeb's return to orthodoxy.</span></span></a></li></ol>
